    "score": 11,
    "max": 15,
    "assessment": "REQ-003 scope is mostly present. The candidate accepts supported extensions up to 20 MB, persists uploaded metadata, writes the original through an adapter, records upload audit before dispatch, emits one processing request on success, exposes GET status, and rejects malformed, anonymous, unauthorized, oversize, and unsupported requests with explicit reasons. Scope fidelity is reduced because the shared-contract requirement is not met cleanly and the failure mode after event dispatch failure leaves a persisted upload without guaranteed downstream processing."
  },
  "lane_fidelity": {
    "score": 9,
    "max": 10,
    "assessment": "This stays in the correct App/backend/python lane, uses FastAPI appropriately, and does not drift into frontend, data, or infra implementation. Deduction is for pulling dependency-slice shared auth/contracts into this REQ payload rather than consuming established shared/common ownership cleanly."
  },
  "file_contract": {
    "score": 10,
    "max": 15,
    "assessment": "Required source, test, README/KIT notes, API docs, and CI helper artifacts are present. The source count exceeds the minimum. The main contract issue is purpose fidelity: REQ-003 explicitly says shared storage, audit, events, and authz must be reused, but the candidate defines local Protocol contracts for storage/audit/events inside backend.documents.intake.contracts and stages shared auth files inside this REQ output, which weakens architectural ownership and promotion safety."
  },
  "source_substance": {
    "score": 13,
    "max": 20,
    "assessment": "There is real implementation substance: request validation, dependency injection, upload orchestration, rollback behavior for storage and audit failure, explicit error mapping, and a working status query. This is not cosmetic. The main source weaknesses are architectural and runtime-integrity related: shared concern seams are locally redefined instead of reused, dispatch failure leaves a persisted uploaded document without guaranteed follow-up processing, and there is no durable outbox/retry mechanism to preserve the business outcome under broker failure."
  },
  "test_credibility": {
    "score": 11,
    "max": 15,
    "assessment": "Tests are substantive and aligned to acceptance behavior. They cover happy path, GET status, unsupported type, oversize, malformed multipart, 401, 403, storage rollback, audit failure cleanup, and event dispatch failure behavior. Credibility is limited because all dependencies are in-memory doubles, there is no proof of integration with canonical shared storage/audit/events/auth seams, and there is no coverage/lint/type/security evidence despite backend lane expectations."
  },
  "docs_code_coherence": {
    "score": 7,
    "max": 10,
    "assessment": "Docs, code, and tests are mostly coherent now. API_REQ-003 documents the implemented endpoints and matches the router/service behavior. README, KIT notes, and HOWTO are consistent with the emitted slice. Score is reduced because the docs normalize staged shared/auth files and local intake-owned adapter protocols as if that were the intended promotion shape, which is at odds with the must-reuse contract in the plan."
